tasks.spec:
Implement `computeFov(tiles, x, y, radius)` in src/fov.js returning a Set of 'x,y' strings, exactly per design §3: a tile T is visible from the origin O when dx*dx + dy*dy <= radius*radius and every tile on the integer Bresenham line from O to T, excluding T itself, is not '#'; the origin is always visible; a wall is visible when the line reaches it; tiles beyond it on that line are not visible. Use the exact Bresenham loop from the design (integer err/e2 form, sx/sy signs, negative dy). Out-of-map coordinates count as walls (defensive only). The function must not use Math.random, Date or DOM.
test/fov.test.js (node:test + node:assert/strict) must compare whole sets, not samples, on hand-built small maps:
- origin is visible even when the origin tile itself is a wall; radius 0 yields exactly the origin;
- an open 5x5 room with radius 2 yields exactly the hand-computed set (including walls at the Euclidean boundary);
- the distance bound: with radius 4 the tile at (3,3) is excluded (18 > 16) and with radius 5 it is included (18 <= 25);
- a single wall in a corridor is visible while tiles directly behind it are not, and a diagonal wall blocks what lies behind it diagonally;
- a radius larger than the map is clamped by the map bounds;
- tiles are returned as 'x,y' strings and there are no duplicates; the set is identical when computed twice (deterministic).

Implement `generateLevel(seed, depth)` in src/map.js returning { width: 60, height: 30, tiles, rooms, start, stairs } with the algorithm fixed in design §6 (rooms 6..12 by bounded rejection sampling with the rectangles enlarged by one tile pairwise disjoint; L-shaped corridors between consecutive room centres, horizontal-first when rng.next() < 0.5; start = centre of rooms[0]; stairs = centre of the farthest room by centre distance, first on a tie; '>' at stairs for depth < 5 and no '>' at all for depth 5, where stairs stays a floor tile). Uses only createRng(seed); identical output for identical (seed, depth); no clock, no Math.random.
test/map.test.js (node:test + node:assert/strict), for seeds 1..25 and depths 1..5:
- width 60, height 30, tiles is 30 strings of 60 characters, every tile is '#', '.' or '>', every outer-border character is '#';
- 6..12 rooms, each 3 <= w,h <= 10, fully inside the border, and for every pair the rectangles grown by one tile do not overlap (rooms never touch);
- start is inside rooms[0] and on a floor tile; stairs lies inside a room, is not start, and equals the independently recomputed farthest-room centre (Euclidean, first on a tie);
- '>' exists exactly when depth < 5 and sits at `stairs`; at depth 5 the stairs tile is '.';
- an 8-directional flood fill from `start` over non-wall tiles reaches every non-wall tile (including stairs);
- two calls with the same seed and depth are deep-equal; the same seed at different depths or different seeds differ in tiles for at least most pairs.

test/page.test.js (node:test, node:fs only, no browser) asserting the page contract so later changes cannot break it silently:
- index.html contains exactly one script tag whose src is exactly https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/three.js/0.160.0/three.min.js and that tag has no type="module" attribute;
- it contains a module script with src="./src/main.js";
- elements with ids hud and log exist (also check inventory, title, help and end);
- every src/href in the document is that three.js URL, a relative path, a data: URI or an anchor fragment — no other external URL, no importmap, no inline <script> body other than the two script tags, and no eval(/new Function;
- src/main.js exists, is loaded as a module, and assigns window.game;
- package.json scripts contain only a test script (no build step) and the dependency fields stay empty.
Failures must name the offending snippet so the fix is obvious. Keep the checks tolerant of formatting/whitespace but strict about the exact three.js URL and the relative module path.

Verify the whole page with the screenshot tool and fix the wiring/layout defects it exposes. Do not change gameplay rules or the core (src/rng.js, src/fov.js, src/path.js, src/map.js, src/game.js, src/content.js, src/events.js) — if a core defect shows up, report it in the summary instead of patching around it.
Runs to make and inspect:
1. index.html with no parameters -> title screen with the game name and controls, no console errors;
2. index.html?seed=123 -> immediate start, #hud reads HP 30/30, Depth 1, Turn 0, the dungeon renders with the player, lit floor and walls;
3. index.html?seed=123&script=<a few movement keys> -> the log reflects the messages and the player has moved;
4. index.html?seed=<n>&script=<a long string of '.' waits> -> monsters reach and kill the player: the death overlay appears and #hud reads HP 0/30 (search seeds offline with the deterministic core if needed);
5. index.html?seed=<n>&script=<keys that pick up an item/gold> -> the log shows the pickup messages; the inventory panel contents follow the state (the screenshot tool cannot press i, so verify that path by code review);
6. index.html?seed=123 at 800x600 and at 1280x720 -> HUD, log and overlays stay readable and never overflow the viewport;
7. confirm window.game exists and its state matches what is drawn (depth, turn, player position).
Fix what you find in the listed files only, keep the console free of errors (the three.js deprecation warning is expected), and make sure everything still works from a file:// URL.

